## Abstract

This dissertation develops a rigorous mathematical theory of the transition from an Earth-launched space economy to a self-sustaining in-space economy. The central object is a two-dimensional slow–fast dynamical system in which cumulative Earth-launched mass \( Q(t) \) drives down transport cost through a Wright-law learning curve, and installed in-space capacity \( K(t) \) evolves under Allee-type dynamics with a cost- and legal-certainty-dependent carrying capacity. The dissertation establishes three results. First, the base feedback system \( dQ/dt = KQ^{\alpha\eta} \) — obtained by composing a power-law learning curve with isoelastic demand — is shown to be a **growth-class transition**, not a bifurcation: the qualitative behavior changes from power-law to exponential to finite-time blow-up as the product \( \alpha\eta \) crosses unity, but the system has no equilibria in the interior for any parameter value. Second, this sharp transition is shown to be an **artifact** of extrapolating isoelastic demand and power-law learning beyond their range: the introduction of a cost floor or saturating demand eliminates the blow-up and reduces the transition to a smooth crossover. Third, the introduction of capacity dynamics with an Allee threshold and a market-activation structure produces **genuine saddle-node bifurcations**, with bistability, hysteresis, and a smooth phase boundary in the (cost, legal-certainty) plane. The legal-certainty index enters the dynamics through the activation thresholds and the carrying capacity, not as a cost multiplier. The dissertation derives the general viability condition for a market with arbitrary cash-flow function, corrects the monotonicity of the critical legal certainty, and replaces elicited Bayesian-network probabilities with computed posteriors over structural parameters. All results are analytical. No experiments, code training, or physical tests are performed.

