## 3. The Learning Exponent

### 3.1 Exponent Identification

**Definition 3.1 (Progress Ratio).** The progress ratio is \( PR := c(2Q)/c(Q) \).

**Definition 3.2 (Learning Rate).** The learning rate is \( LR := 1 - PR \).

**Proposition 3.1.** Under \( c(Q) = c_0(Q/Q_0)^{-\alpha} \), the progress ratio is \( PR = 2^{-\alpha} \), and \( \alpha = -\log_2(PR) = -\ln(PR)/\ln 2 \). Consequently \( LR = 1 - 2^{-\alpha} \).

*Proof.* \( c(2Q)/c(Q) = (2Q/Q)^{-\alpha} = 2^{-\alpha} \). Take logarithms. \( \square \)

**Corollary 3.2.** For \( LR = 0.20 \), \( PR = 0.80 \) and
\[
\alpha = -\log_2(0.80) = \frac{\ln(0.80)}{\ln(0.5)} = \frac{0.22314}{0.69315} \approx 0.3219.
\]
An exponent \( \alpha = 0.20 \) corresponds to \( PR = 2^{-0.20} \approx 0.871 \), i.e., \( LR \approx 12.9\% \).

**Remark 3.3.** The exponent \( \alpha \) is *not* the learning rate. Conflating them is a common error. The baseline used throughout is \( \alpha = 0.32 \).

### 3.2 Cumulative Mass Required for a Target Cost

**Proposition 3.4.** \( Q^* = Q_0 (c_0/c^*)^{1/\alpha} \).

*Proof.* Set \( c^* = c_0(Q^*/Q_0)^{-\alpha} \) and solve. \( \square \)

