## 4. The Base Feedback System

### 4.1 Derivation

Compose the learning curve \( c(Q) = c_0(Q/Q_0)^{-\alpha} \) with isoelastic demand \( \lambda(c) = \lambda_0(c/c_0)^{-\eta} \):

\[
\lambda(c(Q)) = \lambda_0 \left(\frac{Q}{Q_0}\right)^{\alpha\eta} = \lambda_0 \left(\frac{Q}{Q_0}\right)^{\theta}, \quad \theta := \alpha\eta.
\]

The scalar ODE is
\[
\boxed{\frac{dQ}{dt} = K Q^{\theta}}, \qquad K := \frac{m\lambda_0}{Q_0^{\theta}} > 0, \qquad Q(0) = Q_0.
\]

### 4.2 Growth-Class Classification

**Theorem 4.1.** The solution \( Q(t) \) of \( dQ/dt = KQ^{\theta} \), \( Q(0) = Q_0 > 0 \), satisfies:

**(i)** If \( \theta < 1 \):
\[
Q(t) = \bigl[Q_0^{1-\theta} + (1-\theta)Kt\bigr]^{1/(1-\theta)}.
\]
As \( t \to \infty \), \( Q(t) \sim [(1-\theta)Kt]^{1/(1-\theta)} \), a power law with exponent \( 1/(1-\theta) \).

**(ii)** If \( \theta = 1 \):
\[
Q(t) = Q_0 e^{Kt}.
\]

**(iii)** If \( \theta > 1 \):
\[
Q(t) = \bigl[Q_0^{1-\theta} - (\theta-1)Kt\bigr]^{-1/(\theta-1)}, \qquad 0 \le t < t^*, \quad t^* = \frac{Q_0^{1-\theta}}{(\theta-1)K}.
\]
As \( t \to t^{*-} \), \( Q(t) \to +\infty \).

*Proof.* The ODE is separable: \( Q^{-\theta} dQ = K dt \). For \( \theta \ne 1 \), \( (Q^{1-\theta} - Q_0^{1-\theta})/(1-\theta) = Kt \). Solving gives (i) and (iii). For \( \theta = 1 \), \( \ln(Q/Q_0) = Kt \). \( \square \)

**Corollary 4.2 (No Interior Equilibria).** For every \( \theta > 0 \), the ODE \( dQ/dt = KQ^{\theta} \) has no equilibrium in \( (0,\infty) \). The point \( Q = 0 \) is an equilibrium but is not approached from \( Q_0 > 0 \) in finite time.

*Proof.* \( KQ^{\theta} > 0 \) for all \( Q > 0 \). \( \square \)

**Corollary 4.3 (Correct Interpretation).** The transition at \( \theta = 1 \) is a **growth-class transition**: the asymptotic growth law changes from power law (\( \theta < 1 \)) to exponential (\( \theta = 1 \)) to finite-time blow-up (\( \theta > 1 \)). It is *not* a saddle-node, transcritical, or pitchfork bifurcation, because no equilibria are created, destroyed, or exchange stability.

**Remark 4.4.** The local growth rate \( Q^{-1} dQ/dt = KQ^{\theta-1} \) tends to \( 0 \), \( K \), or \( +\infty \) as \( Q \to \infty \) according to whether \( \theta < 1 \), \( \theta = 1 \), or \( \theta > 1 \). It is the behavior at large \( Q \), not at \( Q = 0 \), that defines the growth class.

### 4.3 Critical Elasticity

**Corollary 4.5.** With \( \alpha = 0.32 \), the critical demand elasticity is
\[
\eta^* = \frac{1}{\alpha} = \frac{1}{0.3219} \approx 3.106.
\]

*Proof.* Set \( \theta = 1 \) and solve for \( \eta \). \( \square \)

### 4.4 Blow-Up Time

**Proposition 4.6.** For \( \theta > 1 \),
\[
t^*(\theta) = \frac{Q_0}{(\theta - 1) m \lambda_0},
\]
which is strictly decreasing in \( \theta \) and diverges as \( \theta \to 1^+ \).

*Proof.* Substitute \( K = m\lambda_0/Q_0^{\theta} \) into \( t^* = Q_0^{1-\theta}/((\theta-1)K) \):
\[
t^* = \frac{Q_0^{1-\theta}}{(\theta-1) m\lambda_0/Q_0^{\theta}} = \frac{Q_0}{(\theta-1)m\lambda_0}. \quad \square
\]

**Remark 4.7 (Forecast Fragility).** Write \( \tau := Q_0/(m\lambda_0) \). Then \( t^* = \tau/(\theta-1) \). For \( \tau = 5 \) yr and \( \theta \in \{1.1, 1.2, 1.3\} \), \( t^* \in \{50, 25, 17\} \) yr. Since \( \alpha \in [0.2, 0.4] \) and \( \eta \) is poorly identified, \( \theta \) is poorly identified. The dissertation therefore reports \( P(\theta > 1) \) and the distribution of \( t^* \), not point values.

