## 10. Model Comparison on a Common Observable

### 10.1 The Identifiability Problem

**Proposition 10.1.** The composite index \( S(t) \) is a sum of indicator functions and is therefore a step function. Any smooth model will fail to fit \( S(t) \) exactly. A model comparison on \( S(t) \) is therefore not a fair test.

*Proof.* \( S(t) = \sum_j w_j \mathbf{1}\{X_j > \tau_j\} \). \( \square \)

### 10.2 Common Observables

Model comparison is conducted on a shared observable \( Y(t) \), such as annual launch cadence, delivered mass, or in-space revenue. Candidate models:

| Model | Specification | Parameters |
|---|---|---:|
| Threshold | \( Y(t) = Y_0 + \beta \mathbf{1}\{c(t) < c^*\} \) | 2 |
| Logistic | \( Y(t) = K/(1 + e^{-r(t-t_0)}) \) | 3 |
| Power law | \( Y(t) = A t^\gamma \) | 2 |
| Allee | full \( (Q, K) \) system | structural |

### 10.3 Parsimony Adjustment

**Definition 10.2 (AIC, BIC).** For a model with \( k \) parameters and maximized log-likelihood \( \hat{\ell} \): \( AIC = -2\hat{\ell} + 2k \), \( BIC = -2\hat{\ell} + k \ln n \).

**Proposition 10.3.** The Allee model with \( k_A \) structural parameters is preferred to the logistic model with \( k_L = 3 \) parameters under BIC if and only if \( 2(\hat{\ell}_A - \hat{\ell}_L) > (k_A - 3) \ln n \).

*Proof.* Subtract the BIC expressions. \( \square \)

### 10.4 Pre-Registered Decision Rule

The Allee model is favored if \( BF > 10 \) against both smooth alternatives and BIC does not penalize it excessively. Predictions are pre-registered before fitting.

